send_message:{body:"Dear Margaret, thanks for letting us know. I'm sorry, but we can't let ourselves in today or change the locks. Jonah has the legal right to live in the flat undisturbed. Having a key gives us no right of entry, and we need at least 24 hours' written notice for an inspection unless there is a genuine emergency, which this isn't on what we know. Changing the locks on a tenant would be unlawful eviction, which is a criminal offence for you and for us.

What we are doing: we are sending Jonah written notice of an inspection of the property's condition for Monday 12 October, 10:00-12:00 (more than 24 hours ahead). We'll take photos of the condition only. We have also passed your subletting concern to our legal team. Subletting without your written consent breaches clause 9.3, but it needs evidence (e.g. the Airbnb listing, dates and guest details from your neighbour) and a proper process. If you can send us anything you have, please do. Please don't approach the tenant or change anything at the property yourself.

We will update you after the inspection on Monday. Kind regards, Mare & Co",channel:"email",contact:"margaret@ellisonlettings.co.uk",recipient_name:"Margaret Ellison",recipient_role:"landlord"}
